PNT.registerModule({"id": "m04", "number": 4, "title": "High-Accuracy and Augmented GNSS", "subtitle": "", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Explain the principle behind differential GNSS and why a known reference station is useful.", "Describe the role of correction data and why correction age, latency and distance can matter.", "Explain Satellite Based Augmentation Systems (SBAS) and distinguish their accuracy and integrity roles.", "Explain the basic principle of Real-Time Kinematic (RTK) positioning and why carrier-phase ambiguity resolution matters.", "Explain the basic principle of Precise Point Positioning (PPP) and how it differs from RTK.", "Recognise PPP-RTK as a hybrid family of approaches without treating terminology as universally identical across providers.", "Compare DGNSS, SBAS, RTK and PPP at a systems-engineering level.", "Explain why centimetre-level accuracy does not automatically imply resilience or integrity.", "Identify communications, infrastructure, coverage and convergence dependencies introduced by augmentation.", "Ask meaningful engineering and assurance questions about high-accuracy GNSS claims."], "lessons": [{"id": "why-augment-gnss", "title": "Why Augment GNSS?", "html": "<p>Module 3 showed that a GNSS solution is affected by satellite orbit and clock uncertainty, atmospheric propagation, multipath, receiver effects, noise and satellite geometry. For many applications, ordinary standalone GNSS is sufficient. Others require better accuracy, stronger integrity information, faster detection of faults, or more predictable performance.</p>\n<p>Augmentation and high-accuracy positioning techniques improve the user's solution by adding information, additional measurements, external corrections or more sophisticated estimation. The engineering challenge is choosing an approach that fits the required accuracy, coverage, latency, infrastructure, communications and operational environment.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The central question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">What extra information or infrastructure can we add to the GNSS solution to estimate, correct or bound the errors that standalone positioning cannot sufficiently remove?</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m04-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Explain the principle behind differential GNSS and why a known reference station is useful.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe the role of correction data and why correction age, latency and distance can matter.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain Satellite Based Augmentation Systems (SBAS) and distinguish their accuracy and integrity roles.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the basic principle of Real-Time Kinematic (RTK) positioning and why carrier-phase ambiguity resolution matters.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the basic principle of Precise Point Positioning (PPP) and how it differs from RTK.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise PPP-RTK as a hybrid family of approaches without treating terminology as universally identical across providers.</p></li>\n<li><p>Compare DGNSS, SBAS, RTK and PPP at a systems-engineering level.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why centimetre-level accuracy does not automatically imply resilience or integrity.</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify communications, infrastructure, coverage and convergence dependencies introduced by augmentation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Ask meaningful engineering and assurance questions about high-accuracy GNSS claims.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "standalone-augmented-and-relative-positioning", "title": "Standalone, Augmented and Relative Positioning", "html": "<p>It is useful to separate three broad ideas before looking at named techniques.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Approach</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Core idea</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Standalone GNSS</td>\n<td>Receiver estimates PVT primarily from broadcast GNSS signals and its own models.</td>\n<td>Conventional multi-constellation navigation receiver.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Augmented / corrected GNSS</td>\n<td>Additional information improves or bounds errors in the standalone solution.</td>\n<td>SBAS or correction services.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Relative / differential positioning</td>\n<td>Measurements from a user are related to measurements from one or more known reference locations so common errors can be reduced.</td>\n<td>DGNSS and RTK.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Do not reduce this to an accuracy ladder</p><div class=\"callout-body\">These techniques solve different problems. SBAS, for example, can provide integrity information as well as corrections. RTK prioritises high relative positioning accuracy. PPP removes the need for a nearby local base but introduces different dependencies and convergence behaviour.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "differential-gnss-dgnss", "title": "Differential GNSS (DGNSS)", "html": "<p>Differential positioning starts with a powerful observation: a GNSS receiver installed at a precisely surveyed location knows where it really is. It can compare that known position, or the ranges implied by it, with what the GNSS measurements indicate.</p>\n<p>Because a nearby user and reference station observe many of the same satellites through broadly similar conditions, some error components are correlated. The reference station can estimate corrections that are then supplied to the user receiver.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Differential GNSS (DGNSS)\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m04-image1.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why proximity matters</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Some GNSS errors decorrelate with distance. A correction generated at one location becomes less representative as the user moves farther away, particularly for spatially varying atmospheric effects.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m04-correction-age-and-latency\">3.1 Correction age and latency</h2>\n<p>Corrections are also time-sensitive. Satellite clocks, atmospheric conditions and other estimated effects change. A correction that arrives late may no longer represent the conditions at the measurement epoch.</p>\n<p>High-accuracy architectures therefore need more than an accuracy specification. They may also need requirements for correction update rate, end-to-end latency, maximum correction age, communications availability and behaviour when corrections are lost.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Assurance question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">What happens to the user's reported solution when correction data becomes stale or unavailable? Does the receiver clearly change mode or quality status, or can the application continue assuming corrected performance?</div></aside>"}, {"id": "reference-stations-and-networks", "title": "Reference Stations and Networks", "html": "<p>A single reference station can support local differential positioning. Wider-area or higher-performance services often use networks of continuously operating reference stations. These networks observe GNSS errors across a region and estimate spatially varying correction information.</p>\n<p>Network approaches can reduce dependence on one local base station and model atmospheric and other effects over larger areas, but they introduce infrastructure, processing and communications dependencies of their own.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Reference Stations and Networks\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m04-image2.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m04-rtk-network-concepts\">4.1 RTK network concepts</h2>\n<p>Network RTK services may deliver corrections using concepts such as a Virtual Reference Station (VRS), network corrections or other provider-specific representations. The details vary, but the aim is to give the rover correction information representative of its location without requiring every user to operate a dedicated physical base station.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">System-of-systems view</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A network correction service is itself infrastructure. High-accuracy PNT may now depend on reference sites, survey coordinates, communications networks, central processing, authentication, service availability and data formats in addition to GNSS.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "satellite-based-augmentation-systems-sbas", "title": "Satellite Based Augmentation Systems (SBAS)", "html": "<p>SBAS provides wide-area augmentation using a network of ground monitoring stations, processing facilities and geostationary satellite broadcasts. The system estimates corrections and integrity-related information and broadcasts augmentation messages to compatible users.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Example SBAS</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Region / operator context</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>EGNOS</td>\n<td>European augmentation system supporting European users.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>WAAS</td>\n<td>United States augmentation system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>MSAS</td>\n<td>Japanese augmentation system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>GAGAN</td>\n<td>Indian augmentation system.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Satellite Based Augmentation Systems (SBAS)\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m04-image3.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m04-sbas-is-not-just-more-accurate-gps\">5.1 SBAS is not just 'more accurate GPS'</h2>\n<p>In safety-related applications, the integrity function is particularly important. SBAS can provide information enabling users to determine whether service should be relied upon within defined protection levels and alerting requirements.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Key distinction</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Accuracy asks how close the solution is to truth. Integrity asks whether the system can be trusted and can warn in time when it should not be. An augmentation system designed for safety-of-life use may therefore be valuable for reasons beyond improving average accuracy.</div></aside>\n<p>SBAS performance and supported services vary by system, geography and service definition. Engineers should consult the applicable service definition documents rather than assume that every SBAS provides identical coverage, accuracy or integrity.</p>"}, {"id": "real-time-kinematic-rtk", "title": "Real-Time Kinematic (RTK)", "html": "<p>RTK is a high-accuracy relative positioning technique that makes extensive use of GNSS carrier-phase measurements. Module 3 introduced the carrier-phase trade-off: the measurement is extremely precise, but the receiver initially does not know the integer number of complete carrier wavelengths between satellite and receiver.</p>\n<p>RTK uses simultaneous observations at a reference station and rover to remove or reduce common errors and to resolve these carrier-phase ambiguities. Once a reliable fixed ambiguity solution is obtained, centimetre-level relative positioning can be achievable under suitable conditions.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Real-Time Kinematic (RTK)\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m04-image4.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m04-float-versus-fixed\">6.1 Float versus fixed</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>State</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Meaning</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Float solution</td>\n<td>Carrier ambiguities have not yet been reliably fixed to integer values; precision is generally poorer than a fixed solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Fixed solution</td>\n<td>Integer ambiguities have been resolved and validated sufficiently for the receiver to use them as integers, enabling much higher precision.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Do not equate 'RTK enabled' with centimetre accuracy</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Performance depends on ambiguity resolution, satellite geometry, multipath, baseline length, atmosphere, correction quality, communications and receiver behaviour. A receiver can be operating in an RTK-capable system without currently holding a fixed high-accuracy solution.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m04-baseline-length\">6.2 Baseline length</h2>\n<p>The distance between base and rover is commonly called the baseline. As baseline length increases, the assumption that both receivers experience strongly correlated errors becomes weaker. Atmospheric differences become more significant and ambiguity resolution can become more difficult.</p>\n<p>This is one reason network RTK and other regional correction approaches exist: they model spatial behaviour across multiple stations rather than relying only on a single distant base.</p>"}, {"id": "precise-point-positioning-ppp", "title": "Precise Point Positioning (PPP)", "html": "<p>PPP takes a different approach. Rather than depending on a nearby local base station, a PPP user combines precise satellite orbit and clock information with sophisticated modelling of atmospheric and other effects, typically using dual- or multi-frequency code and carrier-phase observations.</p>\n<p>The user can therefore obtain high absolute positioning accuracy with a single receiver, but classic PPP generally requires time for the estimator and carrier ambiguities to converge sufficiently.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Precise Point Positioning (PPP)\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m04-image5.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">RTK versus PPP mental model</p><div class=\"callout-body\">RTK gains strength from spatially correlated measurements at a nearby reference. PPP gains strength from precise global satellite products and detailed modelling at the user. Both can use carrier phase, but their architectures and dependencies differ.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m04-convergence\">7.1 Convergence</h2>\n<p>Convergence is the period required for a high-accuracy estimator to reach the desired performance after startup, loss of lock or other disruptions. Traditional PPP convergence can be much longer than RTK ambiguity fixing under favourable local conditions.</p>\n<p>Modern services can shorten convergence through additional frequencies, constellations, regional atmospheric information and ambiguity-support products. The exact performance is service- and implementation-dependent.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Operational requirement</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A system that can eventually reach 5 cm accuracy may be unsuitable if the mission requires that accuracy within 10 seconds of startup. Accuracy and time-to-accuracy must therefore be treated as separate requirements.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "ppp-rtk-and-state-space-corrections", "title": "PPP-RTK and State-Space Corrections", "html": "<p>Modern high-accuracy services increasingly blur the simple RTK-versus-PPP distinction. PPP-RTK is a broad term for approaches that combine precise satellite corrections with regional atmospheric and ambiguity-support information to enable faster convergence without requiring each rover to operate against one nearby physical base.</p>\n<p>Correction services may represent individual error components, such as satellite orbit, clock and atmospheric states, rather than providing a single combined range correction. This is often described as a State Space Representation (SSR), contrasted with Observation Space Representation (OSR) approaches more traditionally associated with local differential corrections.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warning\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Terminology caution</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Commercial and research communities do not always use PPP-RTK, network RTK, SSR and related labels identically. When evaluating a solution, ask what correction information is actually supplied and how the receiver uses it rather than relying only on the product label.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "comparing-the-main-approaches", "title": "Comparing the Main Approaches", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Technique</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Typical principle</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Infrastructure / data dependency</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Key trade-off</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Standalone GNSS</td>\n<td>Broadcast GNSS only plus receiver models</td>\n<td>GNSS constellation/service</td>\n<td>Simple/global but limited by uncorrected errors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>DGNSS</td>\n<td>Code/range corrections from known reference(s)</td>\n<td>Reference station + correction link</td>\n<td>Improved accuracy over local/regional area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>SBAS</td>\n<td>Wide-area corrections + integrity information</td>\n<td>Ground monitoring + processing + GEO broadcast</td>\n<td>Wide-area service; strong integrity role in supported applications.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>RTK</td>\n<td>Relative carrier-phase positioning</td>\n<td>Nearby base/network + low-latency correction link</td>\n<td>Centimetre-level capability with strong local/network dependencies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PPP</td>\n<td>Precise orbit/clock + modelling + carrier phase</td>\n<td>Precise correction products/service</td>\n<td>No local base; convergence can be a key constraint.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PPP-RTK</td>\n<td>Precise global + regional atmospheric/ambiguity support</td>\n<td>Correction network/service + communications</td>\n<td>Combines wide-area delivery with faster high-accuracy convergence.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Comparing the Main Approaches\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m04-image6.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "communications-become-part-of-pnt", "title": "Communications Become Part of PNT", "html": "<p>Once a receiver depends on externally supplied corrections, the correction delivery path becomes part of the end-to-end PNT architecture. Depending on the service, corrections may arrive through terrestrial radio, mobile/IP networks, satellite broadcasts or other data links.</p>\n<p>That introduces engineering questions beyond satellite reception:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>What correction transport is used and what happens when it fails?</p></li>\n<li><p>What latency and update rate are required?</p></li>\n<li><p>How are correction data authenticated or protected against corruption?</p></li>\n<li><p>Can the user detect stale, duplicated or inconsistent correction data?</p></li>\n<li><p>Is the service geographically available throughout the operational area?</p></li>\n<li><p>Does loss of communications cause an immediate loss of position, a controlled downgrade, or continued operation using ageing corrections?</p></li>\n<li><p>What external service-level or commercial dependencies exist?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Resilience lesson</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Improving accuracy can add dependencies. A standalone receiver may be less accurate but operationally simpler. A centimetre-level service may depend on GNSS, a correction network, communications, processing infrastructure and service credentials. Better accuracy is not automatically better resilience.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "accuracy-is-not-integrity-and-accuracy-is-not-resilience", "title": "Accuracy is not Integrity, and Accuracy is not Resilience", "html": "<p>High-accuracy terminology can create false confidence. A receiver reporting centimetre-level precision may still be vulnerable to interference, spoofing, bad corrections, multipath, communications failure or incorrect ambiguity fixing.</p>\n<p>Similarly, repeated measurements that agree closely with one another demonstrate precision, not necessarily truth. High-accuracy PNT architectures must still address the Module 1 concepts of availability, continuity and integrity, and later modules will add deliberate threats and source diversity.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Supplier challenge</p><div class=\"callout-body\">If a supplier says 'we use RTK, so our PNT is resilient', the conclusion does not follow. RTK describes a high-accuracy positioning technique. Resilience requires analysis of threats, failure modes, dependencies, detection, fallback and recovery.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "engineering-selection-start-with-the-requirement", "title": "Engineering Selection: Start with the Requirement", "html": "<p>The correct augmentation technique depends on the operational requirement rather than a desire for the smallest possible accuracy number.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Requirement question</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why it changes the design</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Required horizontal/vertical accuracy?</td>\n<td>Determines whether standalone, augmented or carrier-phase techniques may be necessary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Absolute or relative position?</td>\n<td>RTK and PPP solve different reference/architecture problems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Time to required accuracy?</td>\n<td>PPP-style convergence may be unacceptable for some missions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Operating area / coverage?</td>\n<td>Local base, network and SBAS/PPP service coverage differ.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Connectivity available?</td>\n<td>Some correction services require continuous or frequent data delivery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Static or highly dynamic user?</td>\n<td>Dynamics and signal interruptions affect ambiguity maintenance and recovery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Threat / interference environment?</td>\n<td>High accuracy does not address GNSS denial or deception by itself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity requirement?</td>\n<td>Safety or mission-critical use may require monitored bounds/alerts, not merely low average error.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>What happens when augmentation fails?</td>\n<td>Fallback behaviour and degraded-mode performance must be defined.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "worked-scenarios", "title": "Worked Scenarios", "html": "<h2 id=\"m04-precision-surveying\">13.1 Precision surveying</h2>\n<p>A surveyor needs centimetre-level absolute coordinates across a construction site and has reliable mobile connectivity. Network RTK may be attractive because local/regional reference infrastructure can support rapid high-accuracy positioning. The engineering assessment should still consider coverage, correction latency, fixed-solution status and the required reference frame.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m04-remote-wide-area-operation\">13.2 Remote wide-area operation</h2>\n<p>A platform operates over a wide area where deploying local base stations is impractical. A PPP or satellite-delivered precise correction service may reduce local infrastructure needs, but convergence, service availability and correction delivery become key design considerations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m04-safety-related-aviation\">13.3 Safety-related aviation</h2>\n<p>The requirement is not simply 'more accurate GPS'. Integrity, alerting and defined service performance are central. An aviation-certified SBAS service can provide augmentation designed around those needs within its supported service area.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Same technology family, different engineering problem</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Surveying, autonomous systems and aviation can all use augmented GNSS, but their acceptance criteria can be radically different. Never select a technique from the accuracy figure alone.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "speaking-like-a-pnt-engineer", "title": "Speaking Like a PNT Engineer", "html": "<p>When someone claims 'centimetre-level GNSS', useful questions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Which technique is providing that performance: RTK, network RTK, PPP, PPP-RTK or something else?</p></li>\n<li><p>Is the stated accuracy absolute or relative, horizontal or vertical?</p></li>\n<li><p>What statistical confidence/percentile does the accuracy represent?</p></li>\n<li><p>What is the time to first fix and time to required accuracy?</p></li>\n<li><p>Does the claim require an RTK fixed solution?</p></li>\n<li><p>What is the permitted base-to-rover distance or correction service coverage?</p></li>\n<li><p>What correction transport, latency and update rate are required?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens during correction outages or loss of carrier lock?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is solution status, uncertainty and integrity communicated to the application?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which reference frame and height system are used?</p></li>\n<li><p>What test evidence demonstrates the claim across the intended operational environment?</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>Augmentation improves or bounds GNSS errors by adding information, measurements, corrections or external infrastructure.</p></li>\n<li><p>DGNSS exploits correlated errors between a known reference station and a user.</p></li>\n<li><p>Correction latency, age, baseline distance, communications and coverage are engineering constraints, not implementation trivia.</p></li>\n<li><p>SBAS provides wide-area corrections and, for supported safety services, integrity information and alerting capability.</p></li>\n<li><p>RTK uses differential carrier-phase processing and integer ambiguity resolution to achieve centimetre-level relative positioning under suitable conditions.</p></li>\n<li><p>A fixed RTK solution is fundamentally different from an unresolved float solution.</p></li>\n<li><p>PPP uses precise satellite orbit/clock products and detailed modelling without requiring a nearby local base, but convergence can be a major operational consideration.</p></li>\n<li><p>PPP-RTK combines precise global and regional information to support faster high-accuracy positioning; terminology varies by implementation.</p></li>\n<li><p>High accuracy does not prove integrity or resilience.</p></li>\n<li><p>Selecting a high-accuracy PNT approach starts with operational requirements: accuracy, coverage, convergence, connectivity, integrity, failure behaviour and evidence.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>DGNSS</td>\n<td>Differential GNSS: positioning using corrections or relative measurements derived from one or more known reference stations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Reference station</td>\n<td>GNSS receiver at a precisely known location used to estimate errors/corrections.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Rover</td>\n<td>Mobile or user receiver whose position is being determined.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Baseline</td>\n<td>Distance/vector between reference receiver and rover.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Correction age</td>\n<td>Elapsed time between the epoch represented by a correction and its use by the receiver.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>SBAS</td>\n<td>Satellite Based Augmentation System providing wide-area GNSS corrections and integrity-related information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>RTK</td>\n<td>Real-Time Kinematic positioning using carrier-phase differential processing for high precision.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Float solution</td>\n<td>Carrier ambiguities estimated as non-integer/uncertain values rather than reliably fixed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Fixed solution</td>\n<td>Carrier ambiguities resolved and validated as integers for high-precision positioning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PPP</td>\n<td>Precise Point Positioning using precise satellite products, carrier/code measurements and detailed modelling at a single receiver.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Convergence</td>\n<td>Process/time required for an estimator to achieve the desired high-accuracy solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PPP-RTK</td>\n<td>Family of approaches combining precise satellite corrections with regional atmospheric/ambiguity support for faster convergence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>OSR</td>\n<td>Observation Space Representation: corrections represented in the measurement/observation domain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>SSR</td>\n<td>State Space Representation: corrections representing individual error states such as orbit, clock and atmosphere.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>VRS</td>\n<td>Virtual Reference Station: network-derived reference data tailored to a rover's approximate location.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: Differential GNSS</strong><br/>\nOverview of differential GNSS concepts and reference-user corrections.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Differential_GNSS\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Differential_GNSS</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: Real Time Kinematics</strong><br/>\nTechnical introduction to RTK, carrier phase and differential processing.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Real_Time_Kinematics\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Real_Time_Kinematics</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: Precise Point Positioning</strong><br/>\nTechnical explanation of PPP observations, precise products and modelling.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Precise_Point_Positioning\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/Precise_Point_Positioning</a></p>\n<p><strong>European GNSS Service Centre: EGNOS</strong><br/>\nEuropean information on EGNOS services and GNSS augmentation.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gsc-europa.eu/egnos\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gsc-europa.eu/egnos</a></p>\n<p><strong>EGNOS User Support: Service Definition Documents</strong><br/>\nAuthoritative EGNOS service definitions, performance commitments and supported service areas.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://egnos.gsc-europa.eu/documents/service-definition-documents\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://egnos.gsc-europa.eu/documents/service-definition-documents</a></p>\n<p><strong>GPS.gov: Augmentation Systems</strong><br/>\nUS government overview of GPS augmentation approaches including WAAS and differential systems.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gps.gov/augmentation\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gps.gov/augmentation</a></p>\n<p><strong>International GNSS Service (IGS)</strong><br/>\nGlobal GNSS data and precise products supporting high-precision scientific and positioning applications.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://igs.org/\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://igs.org/</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: PPP Fundamentals</strong><br/>\nAdditional technical detail on PPP error modelling and precise positioning.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/PPP_Fundamentals\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/PPP_Fundamentals</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 05 — ALTERNATIVE PNT TECHNOLOGIES</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>The first four modules have concentrated heavily on GNSS. Module 5 deliberately breaks that dependency. It will introduce inertial navigation systems and IMUs, accelerometers and gyroscopes, dead reckoning, clocks and holdover, network timing, terrestrial navigation, signals of opportunity and emerging LEO-based PNT. The central question becomes: if GNSS is unavailable, degraded or unsuitable, what else can provide Position, Navigation or Timing?</p>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "What is the core idea behind differential GNSS?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "A known reference receiver estimates errors/corrections that can help a nearby user."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The rover transmits commands to GNSS satellites."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Two receivers automatically remove all errors regardless of distance."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It replaces satellites with inertial sensors."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "A surveyed reference provides a basis for estimating errors that are correlated with those experienced by the user."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Which can affect the usefulness of differential corrections?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Distance from reference station."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Correction age/latency."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Communications availability."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Spatial variation in atmospheric errors."}, {"id": "E", "text": "The colour of the rover."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Differential performance depends on spatial/temporal correlation and timely delivery of correction information."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "Which statement best describes SBAS?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "A local centimetre-level RTK base station."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Wide-area augmentation using ground monitoring/processing and satellite broadcast of correction and integrity-related information."}, {"id": "C", "text": "An inertial navigation system."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A replacement for all GNSS constellations."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "SBAS combines wide-area ground monitoring and processing with satellite delivery of augmentation and integrity information."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "Why can RTK achieve very high precision?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "It ignores carrier phase."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It exploits precise carrier-phase measurements and differential processing, including integer ambiguity resolution."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It doubles the speed of light."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It uses only one satellite."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Carrier phase is extremely precise, and differential processing plus ambiguity resolution enables centimetre-level relative positioning under suitable conditions."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "What does an RTK 'fixed' solution indicate?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The rover is physically stationary."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Carrier ambiguities have been reliably resolved to integer values for the solution."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The antenna has been repaired."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Corrections are no longer required."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "'Fixed' refers to integer ambiguity resolution, not whether the rover is moving."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "What is a key architectural difference between classic PPP and RTK?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "PPP normally uses precise satellite products and modelling without requiring a nearby local base; RTK uses relative observations/corrections from a nearby base or network."}, {"id": "B", "text": "PPP cannot use carrier phase."}, {"id": "C", "text": "RTK works without GNSS."}, {"id": "D", "text": "PPP provides no absolute position."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Classic PPP relies on precise products and modelling at a single user; RTK relies strongly on correlated observations from local/regional reference infrastructure."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "What is convergence in high-accuracy GNSS?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The satellites physically moving together."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The time/process for the estimator to reach the required high-accuracy solution after startup or disruption."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Converting metres to centimetres."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The receiver connecting to Wi-Fi."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Time-to-accuracy is a separate operational characteristic from eventual achievable accuracy."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "Which dependencies can be introduced by a high-accuracy correction service?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Reference infrastructure."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Communications links."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Correction processing services."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Authentication/service access."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Correction coverage."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Augmentation can make external infrastructure and services part of the PNT dependency chain."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "Which statement is correct?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Centimetre accuracy proves PNT resilience."}, {"id": "B", "text": "RTK automatically protects against spoofing."}, {"id": "C", "text": "High accuracy, integrity and resilience are different properties and must be assessed separately."}, {"id": "D", "text": "PPP removes all need for GNSS signals."}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "Accuracy describes closeness to truth; integrity and resilience address different engineering questions."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "Which should be clarified when evaluating a '2 cm accuracy' claim?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Horizontal/vertical/3D definition."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Statistical confidence."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Required correction service and solution state."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Operating environment and convergence time."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Supporting test evidence."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "A headline number is not meaningful without its definition, operating conditions, dependencies and evidence."}]});
